A||||3||ka4nkf||Fan Switch (ogre)||||1||
Z||000000||ka4nkf||03-26-2001||12:49 AM||||ogre<BR>The 1984 four cyl. had a two stage fan switch. the low stage came on at 218 deg.(low speed fan), and the high came on at 245 deg. (high speed fan). If I took this switch and put it in my V6 and only use the low side of the switch, then my fan would come on at 218 deg. instead of 235 deg. what do you think? am I thinking right or wrong?||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||03-26-2001||07:17 AM||||Does the fan run at the same speed at both settings? if it does, shouldnt be any problem<BR>since it has two settings, ill bet it runs slower on the low temp setting. Im just guessing though.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||ka4nkf||03-26-2001||04:08 PM||||rogergarrison<P>The fan on the V6 only has one speed and that is high. They put the two speed fans on the 84 thru 86 four bangers.Also I got to thinking if you use the four banger dual switch, then you could tie the two terminal wires together and then it would come on at 218 deg and if that switch ever failed then it would come on at 246 deg. The V6 only uses one ground wire green and white. sounds good to me. we will see what ogre thinks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-26-2001||08:13 PM||||Yes. If you put in the 2 speed fan switch then the fan would come on at 218 or 246 depending which contact you used. Yes, you can tie both contacts for redundancy. It won't hurt a thing to do that.<P>To make it look nice you'll want to use the 4 cyl connector and hide the splice up in the OE harness cover if you can. If you can't hide it consider an uninsulated crimp and heat shrink tube. Look more factory and be stronger that way.||||reg||1
